+# ADR-003: Post-Publication Release Binding Audit
+
+**Status:** Proposed
+**Date:** 2026-07-25
+
+## Context
+
+ACP Issue #11 requires every stable annotated tag to have a published Gitea
+release with non-empty notes and the same peeled target commit. The prior
+single-stage evidence model could not bind a release-preparation commit to its
+own unknown SHA. Canonical governance Issue #42 resolved that contradiction by
+adopting committed pre-publication evidence and a separate post-publication
+attestation.
+
+The release audit needs authenticated read access to Gitea, while pull-request
+CI must remain deterministic and credential-free. Runtime deployment state is
+also independent of release publication and must not be inferred by this audit.
+
+## Decision
+
+1. Run deterministic release-audit fixtures in normal ACP pull-request CI.
+2. Run the authenticated audit on a daily schedule, with a manual dispatch
+   immediately after the attestation asset upload and for later re-checks. Do
+   not use `release: published`: the attestation can only be produced after the
+   release exists, so that event can race ahead of the required asset.
+3. Enumerate stable tags from a full local Git checkout and require annotated
+   tag objects.
+4. Require the Gitea release `tag_name` and full `target_commitish` to equal the
+   local tag name and peeled commit. Moving branch names are not valid targets.
+5. Download `release-attestation.yaml` from the release and verify its binding
+   to the tag object, peeled commit, release URL, release-notes hash, payload
+   asset names and hashes, pre-publication evidence, and publication order.
+6. Exclude the attestation from its own `assets` list. It remains a release
+   asset but cannot hash itself.
+7. Use a dedicated repository secret containing a token with only
+   `read:repository`. Code, logs, and review artifacts never contain the token.
+8. Evaluate runtime-consumer pins separately; publication success does not
+   assert deployment.
+
+## Consequences
+
+**Easier:**
+
+- Release/tag drift becomes a deterministic CI failure.
+- The two-stage governance model is directly executable by ACP.
+- Pull-request tests need no network or credentials.
+- Every stable release can be re-audited without changing release objects.
+
+**Harder:**
+
+- The repository owner must provision and rotate a least-privilege token before
+  the first stable release.
+- Stable releases must upload the attestation and every declared payload asset
+  before the audit can pass.
+- Gitea release targets must be immutable full commit SHAs, not `main`.
+
+## References
+
+- Issue: `Homelab/ACP#11`
+- Governance decision: `yeraziael/ai-governance#42`
+- Governance implementation: `yeraziael/ai-governance#43`
